  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "What rated to -30°C really means for a North Bay heat pump",
   "html": "<p>A cold-climate rating tells you the unit keeps running and producing heat at -30°C. It doesn't mean it produces as much heat at -30°C as it does at 0°C. Every heat pump loses some capacity as the outdoor air gets colder, and the good ones lose it slowly.</p>"
           "<p>That's why sizing matters more here than in Barrie or Toronto. We size from the heat-loss calculation at North Bay's design temperature, so the unit covers the house on an ordinary cold January day, not just an average one. For the stretches below -25°C that settle in for a week, the backup heat carries the difference: a furnace in a hybrid setup, electric strips in an air handler, or the stove at the camp.</p>"
           "<p>The payoff is in the other ninety percent of the heating season, when the heat pump runs at high efficiency and the oil tank or propane bill shrinks. The quote shows the capacity at -15°C and -25°C so you can see the numbers rather than trust a label.</p>"},
